[API Documentation]
Fill the 2D input `Tensor` as a sparse matrix.

    The non-zero elements will be drawn from the normal distribution
    :math:`\mathcal{N}(0, 0.01)`, as described in `Deep learning via
    Hessian-free optimization` - Martens, J. (2010).

    Args:
        tensor: an n-dimensional `torch.Tensor`
        sparsity: The fraction of elements in each column to be set to zero
        std: the standard deviation of the normal distribution used to generate
            the non-zero values
        generator: the torch Generator to sample from (default: None)

    Examples:
        >>> w = torch.empty(3, 5)
        >>> nn.init.sparse_(w, sparsity=0.1)

[API Signature] tensor: tensor, sparsity: float

[Error Messages]
OverflowError: cannot convert float infinity to integer
RuntimeError: "normal_kernel_cpu" not implemented for 'Bool'
ValueError: cannot convert float NaN to integer
ValueError: Only tensors with 2 dimensions are supported

[Output Format]
Rule {Number} ({Description})\n{Rule Definition}
Ex) Rule 21 (primitive type variable should not be zero)
    {v_1 : int ⊎ float} |= v_1 ≠ 0

[Example Rules]
Rule 22 (valid step size for a range)
{v_1 : int ⊎ float, v_2 : int ⊎ float, v_3 : int ⊎ float} |= if v_1 > 0 then v_2 ≤ v_3 else if v_1 < 0 then v_2 ≥ v_3 else true

Rule 18 (input tensor is empty or only contains non-negative values)
{v_1 : tensor} |= (∃i ∈ [0, ndim(v_1) − 1] : shape(v_1, i) = 0) ∨ min(v_1) ≥ 0

Rule 7 (first dimension of a tensor matches second dimension of the other)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= ndim(v_1) ≥ 1 ∧ ndim(v_2) ≥ 2 ∧ shape(v_1, 0) = shape(v_2, 1)

Rule 28 (list of ints used as dimensions/axes, all non-negative and valid for tensor shape)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : list(int)} |= ∀i ∈ [0, v_2.len - 1] : 0 ≤ v_2[i] ∧ v_2[i] < ndim(v_1)

Rule 12 (a primitive type variable is smaller than the other)
{v_1 : int ⊎ float, v_2 : int ⊎ float} |= v_1 ≤ v_2

